<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Ganga dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1078–<a href="/years/1434">1434</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern Ganga dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its imperial phase began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantavarman-chodaganga">Anantavarman Chodaganga</a>, who conquered the Somavamshi lands around 1110 and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> Temple at Puri. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i">Narasimhadeva I</a> built the Sun Temple at Konark. The Gangas were succeeded by the Gajapati kings in 1434.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-ganga-dynasty.jpg" alt="Eastern Ganga dynasty" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amshpatten · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Ganga_fanam_Chodaganga.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Eastern Gangas, also called Purba or Prachya Gangas, are named to set them apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, from whom many historians, among them Upinder Singh and Dineshwar Singh, believe they descended. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants trace the line to Kamarnava I, said to have left Kolahalapura, the Western Ganga capital, with his brothers, worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as Gokarnaswamin on the Mahendra mountain and won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>. An early line ruled from the 5th century from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantapura">Dantapura</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukhalingam">Kalinganagara</a>, modern Mukhalingam, often as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of stronger powers such as the Bhauma-Kara dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Vajrahasta V in the mid-11th century the family emerged as a military power that challenged the Somavanshi dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantavarman-chodaganga">Anantavarman Chodaganga</a>, who succeeded his father in 1078, recovered territory lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, took Utkala in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> from the Somavamshi kings and campaigned into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> against the Pala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramapala">Ramapala</a>. He is believed to have ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> to the Godavari, and the Jagannath Temple at Puri was built in his reign. He embraced Srivaishnavism under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>.</p><p>Anangabhima III, who came to the throne in 1211, took the Sambalpur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balangir">Bolangir</a> tract from the Kalachuris of Ratnapura and repulsed Ghiyasuddin Iwaz Shah, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i">Narasimhadeva I</a> advanced as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> in 1245, built the Sun Temple at Konark and was the first Odishan king to use the title Gajapati. After his death in 1264 the dynasty declined: Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> invaded between 1353 and 1358 and levied tribute. The minister Kapilendra finally took the throne from Bhanudeva IV and founded the Suryavamsha dynasty, while branches such as the Khemundi Gangas of Paralakhemundi ruled as local kings until 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kingdom was divided into provinces called mahamandalas under governors, subdivided into mandalas and district-like visayas under a visayapati; each village was a self-governing unit led by a gramika. The king commanded an army with ranks from maha senapati to nayaka, and under Chodaganga a royal navy. The Gangas minted gold fanams marked with a couchant bull, dated in their own anka regnal years, a system still used in the Odia calendar. These coins may be the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> coins dated with place-value digits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eastern Ganga dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1434 Kapilendra Deva, a minister and general of the last <b>Eastern Ganga</b> king, seized the throne at Cuttack and founded the Suryavamsi dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/gajapati-kingdom">Gajapati Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
